Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.23,0:00:04.52,Cap,,0,0,0,,The night before Challenger launched,\Nits engineers said it was too cold to fly.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.29,0:00:07.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,They were overruled.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.74,0:00:09.80,Cap,,0,0,0,,This is the story of mission 51-L.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.53,0:00:20.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,Challenger was the second\Nspace shuttle to enter service,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.68,0:00:23.44,Cap,,0,0,0,,and it first flew in April 1983.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.21,0:00:28.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,By January 1986 it had\Ncompleted nine missions,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.96,0:00:31.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,and mission 51-L was its tenth.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.33,0:00:36.32,Cap,,0,0,0,,NASA had promised that the shuttle\Nwould make spaceflight routine,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.34,0:00:41.30,Cap,,0,0,0,,and in 1985 it was planning\N24 flights a year by 1990.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.99,0:00:44.70,Cap,,0,0,0,,The system was already strained.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.36,0:00:47.59,Cap,,0,0,0,,Nine missions flew in 1985,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.64,0:00:49.61,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the Rogers\NCommission later said even
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.63,0:00:51.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,that modest rate had stretched it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.11,0:00:55.94,Cap,,0,0,0,,Mission 51-L would\Nalso carry a teacher.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.64,0:00:58.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,In July 1985,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.42,0:01:01.54,Cap,,0,0,0,,Vice President George Bush\Nannounced that Christa McAuliffe,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.56,0:01:02.79,Cap,,0,0,0,,of Concord, New Hampshire,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.87,0:01:05.82,Cap,,0,0,0,,had been chosen from more\Nthan 11,000 applicants.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.49,0:01:12.84,Cap,,0,0,0,,She trained with NASA, including\Nweightless flights on a KC-135 aircraft.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.69,0:01:18.24,Cap,,0,0,0,,She was to teach lessons from orbit,\Nto classrooms across the country.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.17,0:01:23.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,Another teacher, Barbara Morgan,\Ntrained beside her as her backup.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.43,0:01:28.45,Cap,,0,0,0,,The crew of seven also included\Ncommander Dick Scobee,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.74,0:01:31.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,pilot Michael Smith, Ellison Onizuka,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:31.64,0:01:35.02,Cap,,0,0,0,,Judith Resnik, Ronald\NMcNair, and Gregory Jarvis.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.81,0:01:39.93,Cap,,0,0,0,,The launch was first planned\Nfor the 22nd of January,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.06,0:01:41.37,Cap,,0,0,0,,and it slipped for days.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:41.80,0:01:44.92,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the 27th, it was scrubbed\Nbecause of high crosswinds.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.77,0:01:53.54,Cap,,0,0,0,,The forecast for that night called for\Ntemperatures in the low twenties,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.56,0:01:56.04,Cap,,0,0,0,,perhaps as low as 18\Ndegrees Fahrenheit.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.67,0:02:01.57,Cap,,0,0,0,,The solid rocket boosters were\Nbuilt by Morton Thiokol, in Utah.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.10,0:02:03.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,Each was assembled from segments,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.94,0:02:06.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,joined by seals made\Nof rubber O-rings.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.87,0:02:12.47,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol engineer Roger Boisjoly had\Nalready warned about those joints.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:13.16,0:02:17.99,Cap,,0,0,0,,In a memo in July 1985, he wrote\Nthat the result of a failure would be,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.06,0:02:22.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,quote, a catastrophe of the\Nhighest order, loss of human life.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:24.05,0:02:28.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,At 8:45 that evening, engineers\Nand managers at Thiokol,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.66,0:02:30.09,Cap,,0,0,0,,at Marshall Space Flight Center,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.42,0:02:33.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,and at Kennedy Space Center\Njoined a teleconference.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.93,0:02:38.95,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol's engineers showed charts\Nof O-ring damage on earlier flights.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.44,0:02:43.03,Cap,,0,0,0,,Their recommendation was not to\Nlaunch below 53 degrees Fahrenheit,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.35,0:02:45.42,Cap,,0,0,0,,the temperature of the\Ncoldest launch before.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.31,0:02:49.39,Cap,,0,0,0,,NASA's Marshall\Nmanagers pushed back.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.94,0:02:52.44,Cap,,0,0,0,,One of them, George Hardy,\Nsaid he was appalled.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.21,0:02:57.47,Cap,,0,0,0,,Larry Mulloy later testified that\Nhe may have said something like:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.98,0:03:00.02,Cap,,0,0,0,,when do you want me\Nto launch, next April?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.87,0:03:05.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol asked for a few minutes\Noff the line to talk privately.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.82,0:03:07.68,Cap,,0,0,0,,The break lasted about 30 minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.67,0:03:11.42,Cap,,0,0,0,,Boisjoly later testified
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.55,0:03:14.80,Cap,,0,0,0,,that a senior manager told Thiokol's\Nvice president of engineering
Dialogue: 0,0:03:14.82,0:03:18.30,Cap,,0,0,0,,to take off his engineering hat\Nand put on his management hat.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.01,0:03:23.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,Boisjoly said that no engineer\Nspoke in favor of launching.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:25.35,0:03:28.82,Cap,,0,0,0,,When the teleconference\Nresumed, at about 11 p.m.,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.84,0:03:31.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,Thiokol's management\Nreversed its position,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.43,0:03:32.68,Cap,,0,0,0,,and recommended the launch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.51,0:03:42.18,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the morning of the 28th, ice hung\Nfrom the launch tower at pad 39B.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:43.97,0:03:47.02,Cap,,0,0,0,,The temperature at launch\Nwas 36 degrees Fahrenheit,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.04,0:03:49.50,Cap,,0,0,0,,15 degrees colder than\Nany previous launch.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:51.29,0:03:54.26,Cap,,0,0,0,,Challenger lifted off at\N11:38 in the morning.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.83,0:03:57.46,Cap,,0,0,0,,Less than one second later,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.84,0:03:59.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,cameras recorded a\Npuff of gray smoke
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.92,0:04:02.60,Cap,,0,0,0,,at the aft joint of the right\Nsolid rocket booster.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.49,0:04:08.48,Cap,,0,0,0,,Investigators later concluded that\Nthe seal in that joint had failed.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.31,0:04:12.34,Cap,,0,0,0,,For almost a minute,\Nthe shuttle climbed.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:13.87,0:04:17.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,At about 59 seconds, a flame\Nappeared at the same joint.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.88,0:04:20.82,Cap,,0,0,0,,Within two seconds, it was\Nstriking the external tank.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.71,0:04:25.55,Cap,,0,0,0,,At 73 seconds,\NChallenger broke apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.06,0:04:27.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,All seven crew members were lost.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:29.57,0:04:30.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,That evening,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.37,0:04:33.25,Cap,,0,0,0,,President Reagan postponed\Nhis State of the Union address,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.42,0:04:35.56,Cap,,0,0,0,,and spoke to the nation\Nfrom the Oval Office.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.17,0:04:38.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,He spoke directly
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.02,0:04:39.96,Cap,,0,0,0,,to the schoolchildren\Nwho had been watching.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.66,0:04:44.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,And I want to say something to the schoolchildren of America
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.00,0:04:46.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,who were watching the live coverage of the shuttle's takeoff.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.76,0:04:51.86,Cap,,0,0,0,,I know it's hard to understand, but sometimes painful things like this happen.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.68,0:04:55.46,Cap,,0,0,0,,It's all part of the process of exploration and discovery.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.27,0:05:04.57,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the 31st of January, Reagan spoke\Nat a memorial service in Houston,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:04.70,0:05:06.60,Cap,,0,0,0,,and read the name of\Neach crew member.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.30,0:05:09.88,Cap,,0,0,0,,We remember Christa McAuliffe,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.98,0:05:13.32,Cap,,0,0,0,,who captured the imagination of the entire nation,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:14.42,0:05:18.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,inspiring us with her pluck, her restless spirit of discovery,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:19.26,0:05:22.98,Cap,,0,0,0,,a teacher not just to her students but to an entire people,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.06,0:05:29.48,Cap,,0,0,0,,instilling us all with the excitement of this journey we ride into the future.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.35,0:05:33.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the 3rd of February,\Nhe named a commission,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:33.92,0:05:36.92,Cap,,0,0,0,,led by William Rogers, a\Nformer Secretary of State.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:38.59,0:05:41.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,Its members included Neil\NArmstrong, Sally Ride,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:41.86,0:05:44.78,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the Nobel Prize winning\Nphysicist Richard Feynman.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:46.37,0:05:48.81,Cap,,0,0,0,,At a public hearing on\Nthe 11th of February,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.18,0:05:51.93,Cap,,0,0,0,,Feynman reported what\Nhappened when he put a piece
Dialogue: 0,0:05:51.98,0:05:54.36,Cap,,0,0,0,,of the booster seal\Nrubber into ice water:
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.89,0:05:56.14,Cap,,0,0,0,,it did not spring back.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.83,0:06:00.07,Cap,,0,0,0,,The Commission's\Nreport, delivered in June,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.32,0:06:02.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,said the cause was the\Nfailure of the pressure seal
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.78,0:06:05.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,in the aft field joint of the\Nright solid rocket motor.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:07.51,0:06:09.53,Cap,,0,0,0,,It called the joint a faulty design,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:09.80,0:06:13.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,unacceptably sensitive to a number\Nof factors, including temperature.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.71,0:06:16.49,Cap,,0,0,0,,It also found
Dialogue: 0,0:06:16.51,0:06:19.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,that Thiokol's management\Nhad reversed its position
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.34,0:06:20.47,Cap,,0,0,0,,at Marshall's urging,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:20.60,0:06:22.51,Cap,,0,0,0,,contrary to its engineers' views,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:22.95,0:06:25.20,Cap,,0,0,0,,in order to accommodate\Na major customer.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.83,0:06:27.76,Cap,,0,0,0,,In his own appendix,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.79,0:06:29.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,Feynman wrote that\Nestimates of the chance
Dialogue: 0,0:06:29.74,0:06:32.33,Cap,,0,0,0,,of failure ranged from about 1 in 100,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.58,0:06:36.28,Cap,,0,0,0,,from working engineers, to 1 in\N100,000, from management.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:37.79,0:06:41.91,Cap,,0,0,0,,He ended with a line that became\Nfamous: for a successful technology,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.30,0:06:45.30,Cap,,0,0,0,,reality must take precedence\Nover public relations,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:45.67,0:06:47.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,for nature cannot be fooled.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.24,0:06:51.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,Was this an accident that did not have to happen?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.72,0:06:53.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,Yes, it was.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:53.88,0:06:58.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,It was an accident that had many, many warnings
Dialogue: 0,0:06:58.64,0:07:02.94,Cap,,0,0,0,,that there was something wrong and that it might sooner or later go off,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:03.62,0:07:05.66,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the warnings were disregarded.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.39,0:07:08.51,Cap,,0,0,0,,On the first anniversary,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:08.53,0:07:09.74,Cap,,0,0,0,,Reagan spoke
Dialogue: 0,0:07:09.76,0:07:13.54,Cap,,0,0,0,,to NASA employees about the lessons\NChrista McAuliffe had hoped to teach.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:15.42,0:07:18.40,Cap,,0,0,0,,Well, she never got to give those lessons in space science,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:19.08,0:07:22.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,but she and the others did teach America's children something nevertheless,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.42,0:07:25.62,Cap,,0,0,0,,something precious, something enduring,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.36,0:07:29.62,Cap,,0,0,0,,something perhaps more valuable than any other lesson they will ever learn.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.25,0:07:33.81,Cap,,0,0,0,,The shuttle was\Ngrounded for 32 months.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:34.28,0:07:38.20,Cap,,0,0,0,,Discovery flew again on\Nthe 29th of September 1988,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:38.29,0:07:39.84,Cap,,0,0,0,,with redesigned boosters.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:41.61,0:07:44.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,Roger Boisjoly left Morton Thiokol,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.30,0:07:47.26,Cap,,0,0,0,,and spent the rest of his career\Nspeaking about engineering ethics.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:50.09,0:07:53.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,The engineers said not to\Nlaunch below 53 degrees,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:53.02,0:07:55.66,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that same night the\Nwarning was reversed.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:56.95,0:07:59.31,Cap,,0,0,0,,When the people closest\Nto the data say stop,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.94,0:08:01.92,Cap,,0,0,0,,who decides whether anyone listens?
